# FRSA-0.1: First-Principles Reconfigurable Spacecraft Architecture

ouadi maakoul and SI TEAM

## Abstract

We propose **FRSA-0.1**, a first-principles architecture for crewed spacecraft in which the mapping between physical hardware and mission function is treated as a runtime-reconfigurable variable rather than a fixed design constant. The architecture is derived from mission constraints, not from any reference vehicle morphology. We formalize three levels of reconfigurability — **CRG-0** (static), **CRG-1** (logical/software), and **CRG-2** (physical-topological) — and define a falsifiable hypothesis with pre-registered effect-size thresholds. We instantiate the architecture around a reference mission \(M_0\) (4 crew, 30 days, LEO ↔ NRHO) and provide the full technical specification: parametric mass budget, resource ledgers, CRG policies, topology-independent fault model, oracle MILP baseline for CRG-1, experiment design, and analysis pipeline. The proposal is explicitly framed as a **synthetic architecture benchmark**, not a claim about flight reliability. Critical review has been integrated: the central result is no longer true by construction, CRG-2 overhead is explicitly accounted for, and the novelty claim is positioned against existing FDIR, bus-tie, and cross-strapping practice.

