## 8. Oracle Baseline for CRG-1

### 8.1 Oracle formulation (MILP)

Given a static physical graph \(H_0 = (V, E_0)\), a fault set \(F_t\), and a function set \(\mathcal F\), the oracle CRG-1 problem is:

\[
\max_{\rho: C \to F} \quad U(\rho)
\]

subject to:

\[
\text{for each } f \in F, \quad \exists \text{ path in } H_0 \text{ from inputs to outputs}
\]

Mixed-integer linear program:

\[
\max \sum_{j} w_j y_j
\]

\[
\text{s.t.} \quad y_j \leq \sum_{i} x_{ij} \quad \forall j
\]

\[
\sum_{i} x_{ij} \leq 1 \quad \forall j
\]

\[
\sum_{j} x_{ij} \leq 1 \quad \forall i
\]

\[
x_{ij} \in \{0,1\}
\]

where \(x_{ij} = 1\) if compute node \(i\) is assigned to function \(j\), and \(y_j = 1\) if function \(j\) is satisfiable.

### 8.2 Oracle gap

\[
\Delta_{\mathrm{oracle}} = U_{\mathrm{oracle}} - U_{\mathrm{heuristic}}
\]

If \(\Delta_{\mathrm{oracle}}\) is large, the heuristic CRG-1 baseline is weak, and \(N_{\mathrm{physical}}\) must be counted against the oracle, not the heuristic.

