## 14. Related Work and Novelty

### 14.1 Existing reconfiguration practice

**FDIR (Fault Detection, Isolation, Recovery):** Spacecraft FDIR systems implement hierarchical reconfiguration levels from unit-level switchover to safe-mode. The golden rule is redundancy: switch to the redundant unit.

**Bus-tie and cross-strapping:** Spacecraft electrical power systems already use bus-tie reconfiguration. The ISS MBSU allows cross-tying of power channels. SpaceWire networks use cross-strapped hubs.

**Modular self-reconfigurable spacecraft (MSRS):** A 2023 review in *Acta Astronautica* covers homogeneous or heterogeneous modules that autonomously achieve configuration transformation. This is a **physical reconfiguration** approach, but at the module level, not the interconnect level.

### 14.2 What CRG-2 adds beyond existing practice

| Existing capability | CRG-2 distinction |
|---|---|
| Bus-tie switches | Runtime reconfigurable **power topology** including load shedding and source rerouting |
| Cross-strapped data buses | Runtime reconfigurable **data topology** including function migration across compute nodes |
| FDIR redundancy | **Alternative architectures**, not just copies |
| MSRS module reconfiguration | **Internal interconnect reconfiguration** without moving physical modules |

**Precise novelty claim:**

\[
\boxed{
\text{CRG-2 = runtime reconfiguration of the physical interconnect graph } H_t
}
\]

Existing practice reconfigures **logical assignment** \(\rho_t\) on a fixed \(H_0\). CRG-2 changes \(H_t\) itself. The scientific question is whether this additional degree of freedom provides recoverability that \(\rho_t\)-only reconfiguration cannot.

