## B.4 Proposition 4 — Oracle Gap Bound

### B.4.1 Formal statement

**Proposition 4.** Let \(U_{\mathrm{oracle}}\) be the utility of the optimal CRG-1 policy (MILP solution) and \(U_{\mathrm{heuristic}}\) be the utility of a heuristic CRG-1 policy. Then:

\[
U_{\mathrm{heuristic}} \leq U_{\mathrm{oracle}}
\]

and

\[
\Delta_{\mathrm{oracle}} = U_{\mathrm{oracle}} - U_{\mathrm{heuristic}} \geq 0
\]

### B.4.2 Proof

**Proof.** The oracle MILP maximizes \(U(\rho)\) over all feasible reassignments \(\rho\) subject to the physical connectivity constraints of \(H_0\). The heuristic policy selects a feasible \(\rho_{\mathrm{heuristic}}\). Since the MILP maximizes over a superset of heuristic policies:

\[
U_{\mathrm{oracle}} = \max_{\rho \in \mathcal R_{\mathrm{feasible}}} U(\rho) \geq U(\rho_{\mathrm{heuristic}}) = U_{\mathrm{heuristic}}
\]

Therefore \(\Delta_{\mathrm{oracle}} \geq 0\). \(\square\)

### B.4.3 Interpretation

If \(\Delta_{\mathrm{oracle}}\) is large, the heuristic baseline is weak, and any CRG-2 advantage measured against the heuristic may be an artifact. The correct comparison is:

\[
N_{\mathrm{physical}} = \#\{\text{faults where CRG-2 succeeds and CRG-1}^{\mathrm{oracle}} \text{ fails}\}
\]

