## B.7 Proposition 7 — Hoop Stress and Wall Thickness

### B.7.1 Formal statement

**Proposition 7.** For a thin-walled cylindrical pressure vessel of radius \(r\) and internal pressure \(p\), the hoop stress is:

\[
\sigma_{\mathrm{hoop}} = \frac{pr}{t}
\]

and the required wall thickness for allowable stress \(\sigma_{\mathrm{allow}}\) is:

\[
t = \frac{pr}{\sigma_{\mathrm{allow}}}
\]

### B.7.2 Proof

**Proof.** Consider a half-cylinder of length \(L\) and radius \(r\). The internal pressure \(p\) acts on the projected area \(2rL\), producing a force:

\[
F = p \cdot 2rL
\]

The hoop stress acts on the two wall cross-sections of area \(tL\) each, producing a resisting force:

\[
F_{\mathrm{resist}} = 2 \sigma_{\mathrm{hoop}} tL
\]

Equilibrium:

\[
p \cdot 2rL = 2 \sigma_{\mathrm{hoop}} tL
\]

\[
\sigma_{\mathrm{hoop}} = \frac{pr}{t}
\]

Solving for \(t\):

\[
t = \frac{pr}{\sigma_{\mathrm{allow}}}
\]

\(\square\)

### B.7.3 Numerical evaluation

For \(p = 101{,}325\) Pa, \(r = 2.5\) m, \(\sigma_{\mathrm{allow}} = 300\) MPa:

\[
t = \frac{101325 \times 2.5}{300 \times 10^6} \approx 0.84 \text{ mm}
\]

With safety factor 2 and buckling constraints, effective thickness is typically 2–5 mm. For a 5 m diameter, 5 m long cylinder:

\[
A_{\mathrm{shell}} = 2\pi r L + 2\pi r^2 \approx 2\pi(2.5)(5) + 2\pi(2.5)^2 \approx 78.5 + 39.3 = 117.8 \text{ m}^2
\]

For aluminum (\(\rho = 2700\) kg/m³) at \(t = 3\) mm:

\[
m_{\mathrm{shell}} = 117.8 \times 0.003 \times 2700 \approx 954 \text{ kg}
\]

This is consistent with the 1.5–2.8 t range in the parametric mass model.

