# Appendix I — Oracle MILP — Full Formulation

## I.1 Decision Variables

| Variable | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| \(x_{ij}\) | Binary | Node \(i\) assigned to function \(j\) |
| \(y_j\) | Binary | Function \(j\) is satisfiable |
| \(z_{jk}\) | Binary | Function \(j\) uses resource \(k\) |

## I.2 Objective

\[
\max \sum_{j \in F} w_j y_j
\]

where \(w_j\) is the mission priority weight of function \(j\).

## I.3 Constraints

### I.3.1 Assignment constraints

\[
\sum_{i \in C} x_{ij} \leq 1 \quad \forall j \in F
\]

\[
\sum_{j \in F} x_{ij} \leq 1 \quad \forall i \in C
\]

### I.3.2 Capability constraints

\[
x_{ij} = 1 \implies P_i \geq P_j^{\min} \land M_i \geq M_j^{\min}
\]

Linearized:

\[
P_i x_{ij} \geq P_j^{\min} x_{ij}
\]

\[
M_i x_{ij} \geq M_j^{\min} x_{ij}
\]

### I.3.3 Connectivity constraints

For each function \(j\) requiring input \(a\) and output \(b\):

\[
y_j = 1 \implies \exists \text{ path } a \rightsquigarrow b \text{ in } H_0
\]

This is encoded via flow variables:

\[
\sum_{e \in \mathrm{Out}(a)} f_e - \sum_{e \in \mathrm{In}(a)} f_e = y_j
\]

\[
\sum_{e \in \mathrm{Out}(b)} f_e - \sum_{e \in \mathrm{In}(b)} f_e = -y_j
\]

\[
\sum_{e \in \mathrm{Out}(v)} f_e - \sum_{e \in \mathrm{In}(v)} f_e = 0 \quad \forall v \neq a, b
\]

\[
0 \leq f_e \leq c_e
\]

### I.3.4 Function-resource constraints

\[
z_{jk} = 1 \implies \text{resource } k \text{ is available}
\]

\[
\sum_{k} z_{jk} \geq y_j
\]

## I.4 Solver

Use an MILP solver (e.g., Gurobi, CPLEX, or open-source CBC). For 8 nodes and 10 functions, the problem is small and solves in milliseconds.

## I.5 Oracle Gap

\[
\Delta_{\mathrm{oracle}} = U_{\mathrm{oracle}} - U_{\mathrm{heuristic}}
\]

If \(\Delta_{\mathrm{oracle}} > 0.1 U_{\mathrm{oracle}}\), the heuristic is weak and must be replaced.

